NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } { As soon as black plays Qxa2, it's a loss. The lines can get complicated, but rely on your confidence that you have already won. The Qxa2 lines aren't that difficult to remember. } Qxa2 6. Qxe8 Qxa1 { We're going in order from left to right: Qxa1, Qxb1, Qxb2. Those are black's three possible moves here. This one is easiest. } 7. Qxb8 { The most popular move for white here is to blow the win by taking on f7. Here's a shorthand to remember this correct move: if the black queen "runs away" into the corner, "chase it" by taking the piece closest to it. That's the knight on b8. } Qxb1 { Qxb1 is black's best move. The other possible moves are shown in the two quizzes in the chapters that follow this one--they're pretty easy wins for white. This one is slightly harder. } 8. Qxa7 { This is actually the only move that maintains the forced win for white. } Rxa7 { Things start to get a little tricky here. If the queen had taken on b2 or c1 instead of Rxa7, it's an easy win for white--you just recapture the queen. See if you can find white's next move! } 9. Rg1 { That's a tough one to remember. If you've made it this far, though, you can close the deal. This is really the only tricky move for white. Just remember: If you can't find a way to punish that queen, maybe you need to play another piece. Keep in mind that you've already won. Do not play a fearful, defensive move. Black can't do anything to you. Be aggressive. Black only has two moves here, and neither are good. You know what to do next. } Qxb2 { If black takes the bishop, you play the same next move. } 10. Rxg7 { Ahh, what is black to do? Nothing, that's what. It's over quickly from here! } Qxc1 11. Nxc1 Bxg7 12. d4 Bxd4 { There are several ways to finish things off from here. } 13. Kd1 Bxf2 14. h4 Bxh4 15. Na2 Rxa2 16. Kc2 Rxc2 17. Be2 Rxe2# *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Qxa2: Qxb1 and Rxa7 — through move 8"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } { As soon as black plays Qxa2, it's a loss. The lines can get complicated, but rely on your confidence that you have already won. The Qxa2 lines aren't that difficult to remember. } Qxa2 6. Qxe8 { This time, black's queen takes on b1, which is by far the least common choice for black. } Qxb1 7. Rxa7 { Mnemonic aid: If the queen tries to "sit down" beside the rook, run away as far as you can! Black has to take with the rook, which he does more than half the time. If he doesn't, just capture the queen on b2 or c1 and the win will be obvious. } Rxa7 8. Qxf7 { That's right, move to your safe space on f7. } *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Qxa2: Qxb1 and Rxa7 — complete line"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } { As soon as black plays Qxa2, it's a loss. The lines can get complicated, but rely on your confidence that you have already won. The Qxa2 lines aren't that difficult to remember. } Qxa2 6. Qxe8 { This time, black's queen takes on b1, which is by far the least common choice for black. } Qxb1 7. Rxa7 { Mnemonic aid: If the queen tries to "sit down" beside the rook, run away as far as you can! Black has to take with the rook, which he does more than half the time. If he doesn't, just capture the queen on b2 or c1 and the win will be obvious. } Rxa7 8. Qxf7 { That's right, move to your safe space on f7. } Qxc1 9. Qxg7 { Black can't take with the bishop, obviously. He also can't take on b2 because then Qxg8, bringing out the rook. All he can do it continue the queen race with Qxe1. } Qxe1 10. Qxh7 { Taking on h7 forces the black queen to take on f2. Otherwise, you recapture the queen and that rook comes out. Qxh7 is the best move because it reduces black's options to a single move. } Qxf2 11. Qxh8 { Yes--you want to continue the queen race at this point. If you keep your queen alive, black can't do anything. } Qxh2 { It doesn't matter what black does. Qxh2, Qxf1, and Qxe2 are all options, but white will make the same next move in all cases. } 12. Qxg8 Qxe2 { Black is not going to take the rook on h1 here, because then it's a straight run through all the rest of white's pieces. Taking on e2 appears to give him options (but it doesn't). } 13. Qxf8 Qxd2 { What if black would have taken the bishop instead? Well, play it out--still a quick loss. } 14. Qxb8 Qxb2 15. Qxb7 *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Qxa2: Qxb2 and the a6 checkpoint"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } { As soon as black plays Qxa2, it's a loss. The lines can get complicated, but rely on your confidence that you have already won. The Qxa2 lines aren't that difficult to remember. } Qxa2 6. Qxe8 Qxb2 { This is the most commonly played 6th move for black. It is also slightly more difficult to play for white than the other lines. Most often, white plays Rxa7, but I think Qxf7 is a little easier to play for white. } 7. Qxf7 { If black plays Qxb1, then white has an easy win--just remember: if the black queen tries to sit beside your rook, run away (Rxa7)! Black's queen will be stuck with one option, Qxc1, which allows white to capture the queen and put an end to things quickly. If black doesn't play Qxb1? Well, it gets a little tricky. We will examine black's most common and strongest move first. } Qxd2 { This is black's most common move. We aren't going to play the most efficient line, which would be white wins in 10 because it's a complicated queen race. You could memorize it, but instead, take black's queen and you have a much simpler win in 12! Which piece will you use? } 8. Bxd2 { Right! There aren't any great moves for black here. Most people play a5, which is an easy win for white. Watch out for the tricky a6! } a6 *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Qxd2: Qxb2 and Qxe2 — through move 8"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxb2 { It's important here that white not move to the "safe space" with Qxf7. Instead, you take the bishop. Why? Because it will allow white to later play either Qxg8 or Qxb8, neutralizing the threat of the open bishops. } 7. Qxf8 Qxe2 8. Bxe2 { Both bishops are now open, which looks scary, but (because we previously took the bishop instead of the pawn on f7) black cannot play b5 or g5 without exposing one of his rooks. } *

