NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxb2 { It's important here that white not move to the "safe space" with Qxf7. Instead, you take the bishop. Why? Because it will allow white to later play either Qxg8 or Qxb8, neutralizing the threat of the open bishops. } 7. Qxf8 Qxe2 8. Bxe2 { Both bishops are now open, which looks scary, but (because we previously took the bishop instead of the pawn on f7) black cannot play b5 or g5 without exposing one of his rooks. } Nh6 9. Qxb8 Raxb8 10. Bxh6 gxh6 11. Ba6 bxa6 { This is white's game now. There is no reason white should lose this. } *

