NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxb2 { It's important here that white not move to the "safe space" with Qxf7. Instead, you take the bishop. Why? Because it will allow white to later play either Qxg8 or Qxb8, neutralizing the threat of the open bishops. } 7. Qxf8 { In this line, black is going to play Qxb1 instead of Qxe2 (which we covered in the related branch). } Qxb1 8. Qxg7 { If the black queen takes on the a file, it's over quickly. Black's only real choice here is Qxc1. } *

