NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxb2 { It's important here that white not move to the "safe space" with Qxf7. Instead, you take the bishop. Why? Because it will allow white to later play either Qxg8 or Qxb8, neutralizing the threat of the open bishops. } 7. Qxf8 { In this line, black is going to play Qxb1 instead of Qxe2 (which we covered in the related branch). } Qxb1 8. Qxg7 { If the black queen takes on the a file, it's over quickly. Black's only real choice here is Qxc1. } Qxc1 9. Qxh7 { Black is in a tough spot here. Taking anything with the queen will allow white to release the h-file rook. The only decent option is to take the rook, which initiates a rook race. } Rxh7 10. Rxc1 Rxh2 11. Rxc7 Rxf2 12. Rxb7 Rxe2 13. Kxe2 { This is clearly a difficult move to find. Notice that white's rook must be captured, whether it takes on a7 or e7. What if black plays Nh6? No good. White plays Rxa7, releasing black's rook. The only move that avoids immediate loss is a6. } a6 14. Rxe7 *

