{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxb2 { It's important here that white not move to the "safe space" with Qxf7. Instead, you take the bishop. Why? Because it will allow white to later play either Qxg8 or Qxb8, neutralizing the threat of the open bishops. } 7. Qxf8 { In this line, black is going to play Qxb1 instead of Qxe2 (which we covered in the related branch). } Qxb1 8. Qxg7 { If the black queen takes on the a file, it's over quickly. Black's only real choice here is Qxc1. } Qxc1 9. Qxh7 { Black is in a tough spot here. Taking anything with the queen will allow white to release the h-file rook. The only decent option is to take the rook, which initiates a rook race. } Rxh7 10. Rxc1 Rxh2 11. Rxc7 Rxf2 12. Rxb7 Rxe2 13. Kxe2 { This is clearly a difficult move to find. Notice that white's rook must be captured, whether it takes on a7 or e7. What if black plays Nh6? No good. White plays Rxa7, releasing black's rook. The only move that avoids immediate loss is a6. } a6 14. Rxe7 Nxe7 { Opening up the white bishop looks tempting... } 15. Kd3 { That's the move. The idea is that white wants to avoid a situation where the bishop takes on a6 and then black takes back with the knight, making it difficult for white to get rid of the a pawn. } f6 16. Kc3 { Yes! That's the right move no matter what black plays: f6, f5, any knight move (except if he moves the knight on b8, which would be an immediate win for white with Rh8), Ra7, or a5. Any move black makes, just open up that bishop. } f5 17. Bxa6 Nxa6 18. Kb4 Nxb4 19. a3 Rxa3 20. Ra1 Rxa1# *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Qxd2: Qxc1 and Nxc1 — through move 8"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxd2 6. Qxe8 { That's right. The white queen will take on e8 in all cases. There are four possibilities for black here. We will look at the from left to right, beginning with Qxb2. } Qxc1 { This is the second option for black from left to right. Instead of playing Qxb2, this time black plays Qxc1. This is actually black's best move, mate in 24 for white. The cool thing about this one is that white gets to end the queen race immediately. } 7. Nxc1 { The most common move for black here is b5, but we know that's not right. Keep in mind: black almost never plays the ideal line. This chapter covers the most difficult way for white to win--it'll typically be a lot easier. } Nd7 { Nd7 is black's strongest move here. Tempting to sack the queen right away so you can get rid of that bishop...but not so fast. If you can remember white's next move, you got this every time! } 8. Qxf7 { Correct! Black is prevented from playing b5 and bringing out the bishop because if white plays Qxg7, then black's dark square bishop is coming out to gobble some pieces for an easy white win. } *

