{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
