{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxh1 { So...this is a win in 38 for white. That's not great news for learning this line. :) But don't worry, it won't be as bad as you think. Black's chances become very limited very quickly. We start normally, with Qxe8. } 6. Qxe8 Qxf1 { Whether the black queen takes on f1 or h2, white will play the same move: go to your safe space. } 7. Qxf7 Qxe1 8. Qxe7 { Right! This is a tough one to find, but now white almost certainly has a quick win locked up. The ONE move black can play that leads to mate in 27 (instead of 10) is the extremely rare Nxe7, capturing white's queen. But almost no one ever does that because it looks like suicide. Provided black takes with the queen instead, the win is actually pretty easy for white. We will look at that scenario in the related branch. } *

