NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxh1 { So...this is a win in 38 for white. That's not great news for learning this line. :) But don't worry, it won't be as bad as you think. Black's chances become very limited very quickly. We start normally, with Qxe8. } 6. Qxe8 Qxf1 { Whether the black queen takes on f1 or h2, white will play the same move: go to your safe space. } 7. Qxf7 Qxe1 8. Qxe7 { Right! This is a tough one to find, but now white almost certainly has a quick win locked up. The ONE move black can play that leads to mate in 27 (instead of 10) is the extremely rare Nxe7, capturing white's queen. But almost no one ever does that because it looks like suicide. Provided black takes with the queen instead, the win is actually pretty easy for white. We will look at that scenario in the related branch. } Nxe7 9. d4 { Did you find that one? Not easy! } Qxc1 10. Nxc1 c5 11. dxc5 b6 12. cxb6 axb6 13. b4 { Getting that pawn out in front of the bishop will payoff big time later on in the game. } Rxa2 14. Nxa2 { At this point, it's really tough to know what to do. Black's best move is b5. However, he could play a lot of different things here that lead to a difficult endgame. It's a win in 20, but it won't be easy. } b5 15. Nd2 Nd7 16. Nc1 Nb6 17. Rb1 Nc4 18. Nxc4 bxc4 19. Ra1 { b2 also works. Like I said, this is a difficult line. Remember, no one ever plays it. Look at the barely covered bishop on f8 that's pointed at white's unprotected pawn. Exploit it! } h5 20. Ra2 *

