{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } Qxh1 { So...this is a win in 38 for white. That's not great news for learning this line. :) But don't worry, it won't be as bad as you think. Black's chances become very limited very quickly. We start normally, with Qxe8. } 6. Qxe8 Qxf1 { Whether the black queen takes on f1 or h2, white will play the same move: go to your safe space. } 7. Qxf7 Qxe1 8. Qxe7 { Right! This is a tough one to find, but now white almost certainly has a quick win locked up. The ONE move black can play that leads to mate in 27 (instead of 10) is the extremely rare Nxe7, capturing white's queen. But almost no one ever does that because it looks like suicide. Provided black takes with the queen instead, the win is actually pretty easy for white. We will look at that scenario in the related branch. } { The only difference from the related branch is that here black plays Qxc1 instead of Qxe2. If black plays either of the other two possible moves (Qxd2 or Qxf2), it's the same as the related branch. White will play Qxg7, leaving the bishop wide open and preparing to release black's h-rook. } Qxc1 9. Qxg7 { Now you can see the difference that Qxc1 made: black has more possibilities for continuing the queen race than he had with the other moves. Don't worry, he won't get away with it! Let's play with black continuing the queen race as long as possible. } Qxb2 10. Qxh7 { That's right--open up that rook! Wait...what if black plays Qxb1 or Qxa2, opening white's rook? Just take the queen. Black will lose the rook vs. rook race every time. } Qxa1 11. Qxc7 Qxa2 { This is a crucial point because black can take the knight on b1 and there's nothing left in its path after that. Either of white's moves works, but I think Qxb8 is easier to calculate. } 12. Qxb8 Qxd2 13. Nxd2 Rxh2 14. Qxa8 *

