ack takes the white queen? Doesn't matter at this point. } Rxe2 16. Qxg8 Rxd2 17. Qd8 Rxd8# *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Early Qxh1: Bxe2 and keep away — through move 8"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 Bxg4 4. Qa4 Qxh1 { Okay, big difference here that deviates from the standard opening line we've played so far. Black has not played Bxe2, but has instead chosen to take h1 with the queen. Black won't do that with his other queen options (Qxa2 & Qxd2) because they lead to quick losses. Playing 4...Qxh1 happens in a paltry 3% of games. Remember, in 86% of games, black is going to take with the bishop before committing to a direction for his queen. We will look at all of these possibilities, though, in the interest of being complete. } 5. Qxa7 { It's a win in 14. The most popular next move is for black to finally take with the bishop. In which case, white should play Bxe2! That veers away from our standard opening where we'd normally take with the knight. Just remember: if the black bishop is still in the middle of the board, you're in a "special situation." Thing'll be a bit different. } Bxe2 { Taking with the bishop is the most commonly played move, so we'll look at that first. } 6. Bxe2 { You take with the bishop--this ONLY applies in games where black plays 5...Qxh1 (normally you take with the knight). It is very highly probable black will play Qxh2 or Qxg1 here. If black unexpectedly decides to play Rxa7, it's a whole different line for white, which really complicates everything (see the related branch). Let's examine the probable next move. } Qxg1 7. Qxb7 { From here on out, you're just playing keep away. Capture black's queen if he plays Qxf2, then you have black rook vs. white queen in a race. } Qxe1 8. Qxc7 { Look at black's 4 possible moves. For 3 of them, white can't immediately recapture (disregarding white capturing with 9. Qxc1, which would clearly be disadvantageous). Only Qxd2 allows white to immediately capture black's queen and get in a race with the rook. If black gives you that opportunity, take it. Otherwise, keep playing keep away. } *

