{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 Bxg4 4. Qa4 Qxh1 { This time, we'll look at what happens if white plays Qxa7 and black answers with Qxg1 (or Qxh2, which will turn out the same from whit's perspective). Black's idea is to extend the queen race--basically, he's trying to make things complicated for white until he can find an opportunity. } 5. Qxa7 Qxg1 *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Early Qxh1: Rxa7 and b4"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 Bxg4 4. Qa4 Qxh1 { Okay, big difference here that deviates from the standard opening line we've played so far. Black has not played Bxe2, but has instead chosen to take h1 with the queen. Black won't do that with his other queen options (Qxa2 & Qxd2) because they lead to quick losses. Playing 4...Qxh1 happens in a paltry 3% of games. Remember, in 86% of games, black is going to take with the bishop before committing to a direction for his queen. We will look at all of these possibilities, though, in the interest of being complete. } 5. Qxa7 { It's a win in 14. The most popular next move is for black to finally take with the bishop. In which case, white should play Bxe2! That veers away from our standard opening where we'd normally take with the knight. Just remember: if the black bishop is still in the middle of the board, you're in a "special situation." Thing'll be a bit different. } Bxe2 { Taking with the bishop is the most commonly played move, so we'll look at that first. } 6. Bxe2 { You take with the bishop--this ONLY applies in games where black plays 5...Qxh1 (normally you take with the knight). It is very highly probable black will play Qxh2 or Qxg1 here. If black unexpectedly decides to play Rxa7, it's a whole different line for white, which really complicates everything (see the related branch). Let's examine the probable next move. } { Black playing Rxa7 is quite uncommon, but it introduces a line that prevents us from playing our "keep away" strategy. It's actually pretty difficult to remember because white has to make a counterintuitive move immediately after its queen is captured. However, what you can remember is that you are essentially doing cleanup. Black has lost, and you can lead his loose queen and rook through all white's pieces if you're careful. } Rxa7 7. b4 { That's it! The counterintuitive move is to play b4 here. } Qxg1 8. f4 { Yes. This is to avoid having to capture the queen on f2 with the king--you're just keeping that queen gobbling up pieces. } Qxe1 9. Ba3 { Great! If black takes the knight, take the queen with white's rook. If black takes the bishop with his rook, take back with the knight and then the black queen can't take anything that results in its immediate capture. And cleanup is pretty easy after this. Easy enough to... See the related branch. } Qxe2 *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Early Qxa2: capture the queen — through move 8"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } { Here, we return to what happens if black veers away from the standard opening and fails to play Bxe2. Instead, black will play a queen move: Qxa2, Qxd2, or Qxh1. Let's move left to right and begin with Qxa2 } Qxa2 5. Qxa2 { That's the one! Rxa2 also works, but (to me anyway) the picture of what to do next is clearer when you play Qxa2. Now black only has one choice. } Bxe2 6. Qxa7 { Now, what happens if black plays Rxa7? White takes the bishop on e2 (doesn't even matter which piece white uses!) and black suddenly has a loose rook pointed at the back row. Not good for black. Black is going to want to play Bxf8 and hope to get lucky. } Bxf1 7. Qxb7 { Great! That bishop on f1 isn't going anywhere, so black now has to bring the rook down. } Rxa1 8. Qxb8 { Why not take the bishop to simplify things? You could do that, but I think it leads to a slightly more difficult cleanup. This will already be difficult enough. In this position, white can use the strategy of "just don't move into a place where black can take the queen--EVER" and it works. If you take the bishop there will be some exchange variations you need to think about. } *

[Event "Wizard Opening"]
[StudyName "Wizard Opening"]
[ChapterName "Early Qxa2: capture the queen — through move 14"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Adapted from Dy1ar’s Lichess study f3NuTHGo"]

{ Train as White. Replay this line from the initial position. }
1. c4 d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } { Here, we return to what happens if black veers away from the standard opening and fails to play Bxe2. Instead, black will play a queen move: Qxa2, Qxd2, or Qxh1. Let's move left to right and begin with Qxa2 } Qxa2 5. Qxa2 { That's the one! Rxa2 also works, but (to me anyway) the picture of what to do next is clearer when you play Qxa2. Now black only has one choice. } Bxe2 6. Qxa7 { Now, what happens if black plays Rxa7? White takes the bishop on e2 (doesn't even matter which piece white uses!) and black suddenly has a loose rook pointed at the back row. Not good for black. Black is going to want to play Bxf8 and hope to get lucky. } Bxf1 7. Qxb7 { Great! That bishop on f1 isn't going anywhere, so black now has to bring the rook down. } Rxa1 8. Qxb8 { Why not take the bishop to simplify things? You could do that, but I think it leads to a slightly more difficult cleanup. This will already be difficult enough. In this position, white can use the strategy of "just don't move into a place where black can take the queen--EVER" and it works. If you take the bishop there will be some exchange variations you need to think about. } Rxb1 9. Qxe8 Rxb2 { If black moves along the back rank and takes c1 instead, things are over quickly. It's harder to play when black takes on b2, but just follow the advice: don't let him take your queen! } 10. Qxf8 { Qxf7 works too. } Rxd2 { No choices for black. } 11. Qxf7 Rxf2 { No choices for black. } 12. Qxg7 Rxh2 { No choices for black. } 13. Qxh8 Rxh1 { No choices for black. } 14. Kxf1 { Great! That's the one little tricky part. Remember, up until now, black has had no choice but to move in a straight line through your pieces. All you had to do was keep away. Now there are several ways to win. You can count it out. } *

